# 📌 24. Common Permission Values

```text
600 → rw-------
644 → rw-r--r--
700 → rwx------
755 → rwxr-xr-x
777 → rwxrwxrwx
```

### 600

```text
Owner  → Read + Write
Group  → Nothing
Others → Nothing
```

Useful for private files.

### 700

```text
Owner  → Read + Write + Execute
Group  → Nothing
Others → Nothing
```

Useful for private directories or scripts.

### 644

```text
Owner  → Read + Write
Group  → Read
Others → Read
```

### 755

```text
Owner  → Read + Write + Execute
Group  → Read + Execute
Others → Read + Execute
```

### 777

Everyone gets:

```text
Read + Write + Execute
```

This is generally dangerous for sensitive files and should not be used casually.

---

# 🔐 25. Why `777` Can Be Dangerous

Suppose:

```bash
chmod 777 important.sh
```

Now:

```text
Owner  → rwx
Group  → rwx
Others → rwx
```

Any user who can access the file may be able to modify it.

Imagine a script running with elevated privileges.

If an untrusted user can modify that script, they may be able to alter what the privileged process executes.

This is one reason incorrect Linux permissions can become a serious security problem.

