# 📌 36. Example — SSH Private Key

SSH private keys are sensitive.

A private key should generally not be readable by other users.

For example:

```bash
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
```

This means:

```text
Owner  → Read + Write
Group  → No access
Others → No access
```

SSH may also reject private keys that are too permissive.

---

# 📌 37. Example — Configuration File

Imagine:

```text
database.conf
```

contains sensitive database configuration.

You don't want every user to modify it.

You could use appropriate ownership and permissions such as:

```text
Owner → administrator
Group → application
Others → no access
```

The exact permissions depend on which processes/users genuinely need access.

The security principle is:

> Give users and processes only the access they actually need.

This is called the **principle of least privilege**.

---

# 📌 38. Principle of Least Privilege

This is an important cybersecurity concept.

It means:

> A user or process should receive only the permissions required to perform its job.

For example:

Bad:

```text
Everyone → Read + Write + Execute
```

Better:

```text
Application → Required access only
Administrator → Administrative access
Normal users → Minimum required access
```

This reduces the potential damage if an account or application is compromised.

